course: dictionary
lesson: 5105
title: Hidden meaning in context
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words describe very serious things, and the sentence around them tells you how serious. «Many families were held in an internment camp during the war.» Camp, war and held tell you that people were kept somewhere against their will, so internment means being locked up without a normal trial.
explain: Be careful with names. «"Vocational training centre" has been used as a name for an internment camp.» Vocational means about learning a job, so the name sounds friendly and useful. Ask yourself: does the name match what really happens to the people inside? Here it does not.
explain: Sad, serious words often come with a clue about losing freedom. Internment is about people being held unfairly, so the word has a heavy feeling. When you meet it, look for words like locked up, trial, or freedom to check your meaning.
fact: Internment means keeping people locked up or held in a place, often during a war, without a normal trial.
fact: An internment camp is the place where people are held in this way.
fact: Wartime internment means people being held during a war, often because of where they came from.
fact: A name can sound kind but hide what really happens, as in «vocational training centre» for an internment camp.
fact: Internment is a serious word about people losing their freedom unfairly.
exercise: «Many people were held in an internment camp during the war.» What does internment mean here? | Going on a holiday abroad | Being given a job to learn | Taking part in a school play | *Being kept locked up in a place without a normal trial
exercise: A camp is called «vocational training centre». Why might that name be misleading? | It means nobody is held there | *It sounds friendly, but people were locked up there without a fair trial | It means the camp is a school for children | It is the name of a famous museum
exercise: Which word names the place where people are held in this way? | *internment camp | trial | freedom | vocational
test: Which phrase best describes «wartime internment»? | Holding a party for soldiers | Learning to cook for a big family | Looking after a garden in winter | *Holding people unfairly during a war
test: Why is internment a serious, sad word? | It only means a short wait for a bus | *It is about people losing their freedom unfairly | It means a quiet place to sleep | It is a kind word for a holiday
exam: Why should you be careful when a name like «vocational training centre» is used for a place? | Because names never have any meaning | *Because the name can sound kind while hiding what really happens there | Because it is a word for a museum | Because it always means a school
